const intro={"n":7,"title":"Racist Violence at the Dance and the Killing of an Afro-Argentine Man","summary":"Now a fugitive and separated from his family, Fierro attends a rural dance. While drinking, he directs racist insults and a mocking verse at an Afro-descendant woman who has arrived with a man. The confrontation escalates: Fierro strikes the man with a bottle, a knife fight follows, and Fierro kills him. The woman grieves, and Fierro leaves. He later reports that the dead man was buried without a proper wake. The narration contains dehumanizing language; its inclusion in the poem does not make the insults acceptable or make the killing justified.","people":"Martín Fierro; an Afro-descendant woman and the man accompanying her, whom Fierro kills; other people at the dance. The dead man must not be confused with the Moreno who sings in the later payada of La vuelta.","themes":"Racism and dehumanization; intoxication and provocation; violence and responsibility; unreliable self-presentation; the treatment of death and mourning.","words":"milonga (rural dance or musical gathering in this passage); pericón (traditional dance); facón (large rural knife); moreno (a historical descriptor for a dark-skinned or Afro-descendant person, varying by context); camposanto (cemetery).","questions":["Who begins the insults, and how does the encounter escalate?","How does the speaker describe the Black man and woman, and what biases do those descriptions reveal?","What can we conclude from the woman's mourning and the account of the burial?","Why must the dead man not be identified as the Moreno in the second part's payada?","How does this canto complicate the image of Fierro as only a victim of persecution?"],"nums":[194,199,216],"analysis":"Notice the difference between narrative sympathy and moral justification. The same speaker who tells of persecution also initiates racist provocation, hits the man with a bottle, and kills him. The insulting stanza is quoted as documentary evidence of the narrator's racism, not as an endorsed view. His later comments about the corpse do not erase the harm done. This canto is essential for reading the later advice against violence and the sequel's Moreno encounter critically.","related":"/en/characters/","relatedTitle":"Character guide: distinguish the dead man from the payador"};
---
